.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
SSH_MSG_KEXINIT (RFC 4253 section 7.1) and algorithm negotiation.
.PP
Negotiation is the RFC's rule \- the first algorithm of the client's list that is also on the server's \- restricted to the policy: a name that policy.h does not know is never chosen, and cannot be chosen by being listed first.  There is no fallback.  What would be negotiated elsewhere and is fixed here:
.PP
.nf
.RS
MAC lists      empty when we send them and ignored when received:
               every cipher is AEAD (RFC 5647 section 5.1)
compression    "none" must be listed by the peer
languages      ignored
first_kex_packet_follows   we never guess, and refuse a peer that does
.RE
.fi
.PP
Strict key exchange (OpenSSH PROTOCOL, section "transport: strict key exchange extension") is required in the first key exchange: the client lists kex\-strict\-c\-v00@openssh.com and the server kex\-strict\-s\-v00@ openssh.com.  A peer without it is refused rather than served without the Terrapin protection.  RFC 8308 extension negotiation (ext\-info\-c, ext\-info\-s) is noted when offered.  Both markers belong to the first KEXINIT only.
.SH "TYPES AND CONSTANTS"
